HCL Fundamentals
Objectives
Design and implement a complete HCL configuration system using variables, locals, and outputs with validation constraints
Build a multi-resource configuration that uses for_each, count, and dynamic block patterns to eliminate repetition
Produce a deployable Terraform project that passes terraform validate and generates correct output artifacts from a single terraform apply
Prerequisites
Comfort with Linux command-line operations including file navigation, text editing, and reading command output
Familiarity with key-value data structures and basic programming concepts such as conditionals and loops
Lab Environment
You will work on a dedicated AWS EC2 Ubuntu instance provided by Al Nafi. The instance has a base Ubuntu installation; you will install all required tools in Task 1.

Task 1: Install and Verify the Terraform Toolchain
Problem statement: Your Ubuntu instance has no tooling. You must install Terraform from the official HashiCorp APT repository, confirm the binary is reachable on your PATH, and confirm the HCL formatter and validator both execute without error before writing a single line of configuration.

Requirement 1: Install Terraform using the HashiCorp signed APT repository and verify the installed version is 1.5.0 or higher by running terraform version. The command must exit with code 0 and print a version string â not an error page or a "command not found" message.

sudo apt-get update -y
sudo apt-get install -y gnupg software-properties-common curl

curl -fsSL https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com/gpg \
  | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg

printf 'deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg] https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com %s main\n' \
  "$(lsb_release -cs)" \
  | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hashicorp.list

sudo apt-get update -y
sudo apt-get install -y terraform

terraform version
Official installation guide (consult this if the URL above changes): https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/install

Troubleshoot this step:

You may see E: Malformed entry 1 in list file /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hashicorp.list if the printf command wrote a literal backslash into the file.
Inspect the file with cat /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hashicorp.list â it must be a single unbroken line; delete the file with sudo rm /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hashicorp.list and re-run the printf command if it is not.
Official reference: https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/install
Requirement 2: Create a working directory at ~/hcl-lab containing an empty file named main.tf, run terraform init inside that directory, and confirm the command prints "Terraform has been successfully initialized" before proceeding to Task 2.

Acceptance criteria:

terraform version prints a version string of 1.5.0 or higher and exits with code 0.
terraform init inside ~/hcl-lab exits with code 0 and prints the initialization success message.
Task 2: Design a Variable-Driven Configuration with Locals and Outputs
Problem statement: Design and implement a Terraform configuration that models a fictional multi-environment infrastructure project using only the local provider (no cloud credentials required). The configuration must accept all environment-specific values through declared input variables, derive computed values exclusively through locals blocks, and expose a structured summary through output blocks. No hard-coded environment names, counts, or paths may appear outside of variable default values.

The local provider (hashicorp/local, version ~> 2.4) writes files to disk â use local_file resources to produce output artifacts that can be inspected after terraform apply.

Requirement 1: Declare input variables that together describe: a project name (string), a target environment constrained by validation to exactly the values development, staging, or production (string), a map of service names to objects each containing a port number and a replica count (map of objects), and a list of at least two deployment regions (list of strings). Every variable must include a description and a default so the configuration applies without a terraform.tfvars file.

Requirement 2: Implement a locals block that derives at minimum: a common tag map merged from the project name, environment, and a formatted timestamp; a flattened, iterable structure that pairs every service with every region (producing one entry per service-region combination); and a conditional expression that sets replica count to three times the declared value when the environment is production and leaves it unchanged otherwise. Expose the flattened structure, the effective replica counts per service, and the merged tag map as named output values. At least one output must be marked sensitive = false explicitly and one must use a for expression in its value field.

Acceptance criteria:

terraform validate exits with code 0 and prints "Success! The configuration is valid."
terraform apply -auto-approve exits with code 0, and terraform output -json prints a JSON object containing all declared outputs with non-null values.
Task 3: Build a Multi-Resource Configuration Using for_each, count, and Dynamic Patterns
Problem statement: Extend the configuration from Task 2 into a complete project that generates a set of structured file artifacts on disk â one configuration file per service-region pair, one deployment script per region, and one consolidated monitoring manifest â using for_each and count to eliminate all manual repetition. The project must also demonstrate a dynamic content pattern by constructing the monitoring manifest's scrape targets programmatically from the services map rather than listing them by hand.

A "deployment script" for this lab is a local_file resource whose content is produced by a templatefile() call referencing a .tpl file you author; it does not need to be an executable shell script, but it must contain region-specific and service-specific values interpolated from variables and locals.

Requirement 1: Use for_each over the flattened service-region structure from Task 2 to create one local_file resource per combination, writing a JSON file to output/<region>/<service>.json containing the service name, region, port, and effective replica count. Use count over the regions list to create one local_file deployment script per region at output/scripts/deploy-<region>.sh, where the content is rendered from a templatefile() call and includes the list of service names for that region. No resource block may reference a specific service name or region name as a string literal.

Requirement 2: Create a single local_file resource at output/monitoring/scrape-config.json whose content is built using jsonencode() and a for expression that iterates over the services map to produce one scrape target object per service containing the service name, port, health-check path (add a health_check field to your service variable object if not already present), and a list of fully qualified target addresses in the format <service>.<region>.internal for every region. The file must reflect the actual variable values â changing the services variable default and re-applying must produce a different file without any change to the resource block itself.

Acceptance criteria:

After terraform apply -auto-approve, the command find output/ -type f | wc -l prints a number equal to (number of service-region pairs) + (number of regions) + 2 (the monitoring file plus the monitoring directory placeholder), confirming every expected file was created.
terraform plan after a successful apply prints "No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration." confirming the configuration is idempotent.
Expected Outcomes
A fully validated Terraform project in ~/hcl-lab that applies cleanly from scratch, generates all file artifacts in the correct directory structure, and produces structured JSON output from terraform output -json.
Demonstrated mastery of HCL's type system, expression language, and meta-arguments by producing a configuration where adding a new service or region to a variable default automatically propagates through every resource and output without any other edits.
Conclusion
This lab required you to reason about HCL as a typed expression language rather than a templating system, forcing every computed value through the variable-locals-output pipeline and every repeated resource through for_each or count. The constraint of using only the local provider removed cloud-credential complexity so you could focus entirely on HCL semantics. The patterns you applied here â flattening nested structures for for_each, conditional scaling through locals, and programmatic content generation with templatefile and jsonencode â transfer directly to real provider resources such as aws_instance, azurerm_virtual_machine, and google_compute_instance.
